<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teanum Sidicinum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Teano</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teanum Sidicinum, modern Teano in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, was the chief town of the Oscan-speaking Sidicini, set in the hills between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roccamonfina">Roccamonfina</a> volcano and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volturno">Volturno</a>. Their conflict with the Samnites in 343 BCE drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> into its first war with Samnium. It later became a Roman town on the Via Latina, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and other remains survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teanum-sidicinum.jpg" alt="Teanum Sidicinum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Specializedturin at Italian Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teanopanorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Teano, the ancient Teanum Sidicinum, is a town in the province of Caserta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, about 30 kilometres northwest of Caserta on the main railway line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It stands at the southeast foot of an extinct volcano, Rocca Monfina. Strabo calls Teanum the most important town on the Via Latina, joined by a branch road from Suessa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Teanum Sidicinum was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> tribe of the Sidicini, who drove the Aurunci from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roccamonfina">Roccamonfina</a>. They probably submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 334 BCE, and their troops were grouped with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> in the Roman army. Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cales">Cales</a>, Teanum kept the right of coinage, and like Suessa and Cales it remained faithful to Rome in both the Hannibalic and the Social wars. It was apparently made a colony by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, not by Augustus.</p><p>In the 4th century Teano became the seat of a diocese, and it was later an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> county, part of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. Monks from Montecassino took refuge there when their abbey was destroyed in 883, and one of the first documents in the vernacular Italian was issued there in 963. On 26 October 1860 Teano was the site of the Handshake of Teano, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>, having wrested the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a> from the Neapolitan Bourbons, shook hands with Victor Emanuel II, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, and hailed him as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Roman remains include a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the 2nd century BCE, rebuilt in the 2nd century CE, once one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at 85 metres in diameter, and extensive baths known as Le Caldarelle. The cathedral of St Clement, begun around 1050 and finished in 1116, uses Corinthian columns taken from the ancient town. The castle, built by the dukes of Sessa in the 15th century on a fortress of the 4th century BCE, served as a prison in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> era. The cathedral is the see of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Teano-Calvi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the encounter of <b>Teano</b> Garibaldi met King Victor Emmanuel and handed him the conquered kingdom; the people voted in a plebiscite to join the Kingdom of Sardinia, and the annexation completed the first phase of Italian unification, the Kingdom of Italy being proclaimed in 1861.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sidicini lived around <b>Teanum Sidicinum</b> (modern Teano) in the hills between the Roccamonfina volcano and the Volturno, bordering the Aurunci and Campanians.</blockquote><a href="/state/sidicini">Sidicini</a></li></ul></section>
